77s|n|An inlet of the sea, usually smaller than a gulf, but of the same general character. ; A small body of water set off from the main body; as a compartment containing water for a wheel; the portion of a canal just outside of the gates of a lock, etc. ; A recess or indentation shaped like a bay. ; A principal compartment of the walls, roof, or other part of a building, or of the whole building, as marked off by the buttresses, vaulting, mullions of a window, etc.; one of the main divisions of any structure, as the part of a bridge between two piers. ; A compartment in a barn, for depositing hay, or grain in the stalks. ; A kind of mahogany obtained from Campeachy Bay. ; A berry, particularly of the laurel. ; The laurel tree (Laurus nobilis). Hence, in the plural, an honorary garland or crown bestowed as a prize for victory or excellence, anciently made or consisting of branches of the laurel. ; A tract covered with bay trees. ; A bank or dam to keep back water.	a:1o6h,118,2~1o6h,118,2	n:206e,144,7;vts,132,5;1o6h,128,4~206e,144,7;vts,132,5;1o6h,128,4	N:4t9,78,17~4t9,78,17
77s|v|To bark, as a dog with a deep voice does, at his game. ; To bark at; hence, to follow with barking; to bring or drive to bay; as, to bay the bear. ; Deep-toned, prolonged barking. ; A state of being obliged to face an antagonist or a difficulty, when escape has become impossible. ; To bathe. ; To dam, as water; -- with up or back.	o:4t9,60,5~4t9,60,5	s:vts,137,7~vts,137,7
77s|a|Reddish brown; of the color of a chestnut; -- applied to the color of horses.	j:4t9,80,20~4t9,80,20
